## 10. Failure Modes

**10.1 Claim instability** (highest severity). If claims cannot be individuated as stable units, there is nothing to govern. *Detection:* the same nominal claim yielding inconsistent Reliability or fan-out estimates under different probing methods. Tested first (T0.1).

**10.2 False promotion.** Correlated corroboration evading independence weighting; retrieval-conditioned accuracy confounded by co-occurring useful claims.

**10.3 False demotion.** A correct incumbent overturned by a well-corroborated wrong challenger. *Mitigations:* hysteresis; shared-domain-only adjudication; shadow-store regression sets.

**10.4 Provenance self-reference.** System-generated content entering the provenance graph as an apparently independent source, defeating the mechanism designed to prevent exactly that (§7.1). *Mitigation:* mark system-generated content at the provenance layer and exclude it from independence counts by construction.

**10.5 Shadow-store drift.** The regression source becomes stale or narrows to cover only protected claims. *Detection:* refresh-cadence monitoring plus KL divergence between shadow and serving distributions (§8.6).

**10.6 Context spoofing.** A claim acquires a narrow applicability domain to evade adjudication, or artificially diverse contexts defeat independence weighting. *Mitigation:* scope from observed invocation only; independence as provenance connectivity rather than surface dissimilarity.

**10.7 Rare-knowledge attrition.** Correct but rarely-retrieved claims accumulate empty domains, low measured Reliability, and falling Utilization, and decay relaxes their protection because nothing needed them (§5.2). *Mitigation:* rarity floor with external annotation — imperfect, since it depends on outside labeling.

**10.8 Invisible pretraining conflict.** Per §3.3(2). *Mitigation:* lazy materialization via retrieval-suppressed comparison and ablation localization (T1.4).

**10.9 Abstraction corruption.** A wrong abstraction propagates error downstream while destroying the evidence needed to detect it — the only silent corruption mode here. *Mitigations:* source retention at reduced protection; scheduled re-derivation checks; revert-and-demote on a failed check (§6.4).

**10.10 Procedural leakage.** Procedural content extracted as structured notes enters the declarative store and is governed by machinery that does not apply to it (§1.2). *Mitigation:* a procedural-content filter, unspecified.

**10.11 Oscillation.** Competing claims alternate near threshold at claim level. *Mitigation:* hysteresis; status-change frequency as a health metric.

**10.12 Graph fragmentation.** Sparse or uneven edges leave regions with no usable dependency structure, so regression construction and blast-radius estimation silently degrade to guesses. *Mitigation:* per-region density monitoring; **refuse high-consequence operations in under-connected regions** rather than proceeding on a degraded query.

**10.13 Compute starvation.** Under sustained load, budget-adaptive thresholds rise indefinitely and consolidation stops silently while the system appears healthy. *Mitigation:* budget floor; alarm on sustained threshold elevation.

**10.14 Saturation.** Protection accumulates until nothing can change, or governance cost dominates retrieval cost. Now visible in the objective (§3.1) rather than only as a failure mode.

**10.15 Adversarial consolidation** — treated as a bounded risk rather than a preventable failure; see §13.3.

